      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/0183b9256896263aadfb0d18ae0a88aa11152c6e7e7d5693771f2e5bac20ac0a.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/31c5930bbeca76437d8e7a642bf52da4814bc5afda681b7974b383d3b8762c21.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/c4092a81a26d34360480e2ee46e99a17fa740b398038f36de427f3ed839bc68b.png)",
